<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</h1><p class="st-meta">1900 CE – 1914 CE · capital Zungeru</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British protectorate built on the conquest of the Sokoto Caliphate, where Frederick Lugard developed the system of indirect rule through the emirs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 1 January 1900 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> took over the northern lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-niger-company-territories">Royal Niger Company</a> and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-nigeria-protectorate">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a> as high commissioner. Its capital was first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokoja">Lokoja</a> and from 1902 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zungeru">Zungeru</a>. Between 1900 and 1903 British-led troops conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> in 1903.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>With few officials and little money, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Lugard</a> governed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> who accepted British authority, keeping their courts, taxes and officials under the supervision of British residents. This <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>, set out in his Political Memoranda, became a model for British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Christian missions were restricted in Muslim areas, and Western schooling spread slowly. A revolt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satiru-uprising">Satiru</a> in 1906 was crushed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Lugard</a> returned as governor of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> in 1912 and merged them on 1 January 1914 into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colonial-nigeria">Colonial Nigeria</a>. The north remained a separate administrative region, and the legacy of separate development shaped Nigerian politics.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria.jpg" alt="Protectorate of Northern Nigeria" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Northern_Nigeria_Protectorate_(1900%E2%80%931914).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1900 CE – 1914 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zungeru</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick Lugard, Percy Girouard</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hausa, Fulfulde, Kanuri, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Nigeria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Northern Nigeria, Northern Nigeria Protectorate</dd></dl></aside></div>
